  "transcript": "There's a scene in Fullmetal Alchemist where Ed and Al realize the Philosopher's Stone is made of human souls. Even though they know the Philosopher's Stone would help them greatly and basically solve all their problems, they immediately conclude they would never use it to get their bodies back. This scene reminds me of where we're at with the current state of AI. People are aware that the advancement of technology can bring about good things. But a lot of people also don't just care about technological advancement for advancement's sake. People see what's happening in the DR Congo with kids dying in cobalt mines. Or in neighborhoods in Georgia where people's water is literal sludge because of data centers. Or in India where factory workers are using headsets to train AI on how to do their jobs. People also understand the frustration that artists, musicians, and authors feel when they feel like they're getting their work stolen with zero compensation. And they actually care enough to realize that maybe the current way we're going about doing all of this stuff isn't the best way forward. Ed and Al still move towards their original goal of getting their bodies back. But they always keep in mind how their actions might affect others. And that is something I think the AI industry can learn a little bit from.",
